    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Selecting a single column\n",
        "\n",
        "Selecting a single column by column name is the simplest case for selecting data in a data frame. The general syntax to do this is:\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df['column_name']\n",
        "```\n",
        "Notice the column name is given as a string inside the square brackets. \n",
        "This is an example of **label-based subsetting**, which means we want to select data from our data frame using the *names* of the columns, *not their position*. When we select rows or columns using their position, we are doing **position-based subsetting**. We'll see some methods to do this when we move into selecting rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example \n",
        " Suppose we are interested in the number of bird species observed at the Mugu Lagoon in spring. We can access that single column in this way:"
      ],
      "id": "64be320e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select a single column by using square brackets []\n",
        "mul_spring = df['MUL_spring']\n",
        "\n",
        "# Print first five elements in this column\n",
        "mul_spring.head()"
      ],
      "id": "62a64f57",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Since we only selected a single column, `mul_spring` is a `pandas.Series`:"
      ],
      "id": "682b384c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Check the type of the output\n",
        "print(type(mul_spring))"
      ],
      "id": "32ae50df",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::{.callout-note}\n",
        "## `pd.DataFrame` = dictionary of columns\n",
        "Remember we can think of a `pandas.DataFrame` as a dictionary of its columns? Then we can access a single column using the column name as the key, just like we would do in a dictionary. That is the syntax we just used: `df['column_name']`.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "We can also do label-based subsetting of a single column using attribute syntax:\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df.column_name\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, to see the head of the `MUL_spring` column we would do:"
      ],
      "id": "124fc381"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.MUL_spring.head()"
      ],
      "id": "1755f2df",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::{.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Favor `df['column_name']` instead of `df.column_name`\n",
        "In general, it is better to use the `df['column_name']` syntax. A couple of reasons why are:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `df['column_name']` can take in any column name, while `df.column_name` only works if the column name has no spaces or special characters\n",
        "- `df['column_name']` avoids conflicts with `pd.DataFrame` methods and attributes. For example, if `df` has a column named `count`, it's ambiguous whether `df.count` is referring to the [`count()` method](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.count.html) or the `count` column.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Selecting multiple columns...\n",
        "\n",
        "### ... using a list of column names\n",
        "\n",
        "We can select multiple columns in a single call by passing a list with the column names to the square brackets `[]`:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[['column_1', 'column_10', 'column_245']]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Notice there are double square brackets. This is because we are passing the list of names `['column_1', 'column_10', 'column_245']` to the selection brackets `[]`. \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Check-in\n",
        "Is this an example of label-based selection or position-based selection?\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "If we want to look at the species in the Tijuana Estuary during winter and fall, then we can select these columns like this:"
      ],
      "id": "a50f15a1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select columns with names \"TJE_winter\" and \"TJE_fall\"\n",
        "tje_wf = df[['TJE_winter','TJE_fall']]"
      ],
      "id": "a27184eb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Notice there are double square brackets. This is because we are passing the list of names `['TJE_winter','TJE_fall']` to the selection brackets `[]`. \n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Check-in\n",
        "What is the type and shape of the `tje_wf` output? Verify your answer."
      ],
      "id": "ac12c74a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false \n",
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "# Check the type of the resulting selection\n",
        "print(type(tje_wf))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Check the shape of the selection\n",
        "print((tje_wf.shape))"
      ],
      "id": "f24a28dc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### ... using a slice\n",
        "\n",
        "To select a slice of the columns we will use a special case of **`loc` selection** (we'll cover the general one by the end of the lesson). The syntax will be\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df.loc[ : , 'column_start':'column_end']\n",
        "```\n",
        "where `column_start` and `column_end` are, respectively, the starting point and endpoint of the column slice we want to subset from the data frame. \n",
        "\n",
        "Notice two things:\n",
        "\n",
        "- the first value passed to `loc` is used for selecting rows, using a colon `:` as the row-selection parameter means \"select all the rows\"\n",
        "- the slice of the data frame we'll obtain includes both endpoints of the slice `'column_start':'column_end'`. In other words, we'll get the `column_start` column *and* the `column_end` column. This is different from how slicing works in base Python and NumPy, where the endpoint is not included.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's select the slice of columns that covers all data from Carpinteria Salt Marsh and Mugu Lagoon. This corresponds to all columns between `CSM_winter` and `MUL_fall`."
      ],
      "id": "bff8fb02"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select columns between 'CSM_winter' and 'MUL_fall'\n",
        "csm_mul = df.loc[:,'CSM_winter':'MUL_fall']\n",
        "csm_mul.head()"
      ],
      "id": "b0098a04",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "<!-- TODO \n",
        "### ... using a condition\n",
        "fall_columns = df.filter(like='fall')\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "## Selecting rows...\n",
        "Now that we are familiar with some methods for selecting columns, let's move on to selecting rows. \n",
        "\n",
        "### ... using a condition\n",
        "Selecting rows that satisfy a particular condition is one of the most usual kinds of row subsetting. The general syntax for this type of selection is \n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[condition_on_rows]\n",
        "```\n",
        "That `condition_on_rows` can be a myriad of things. Let's see some usual scenarios.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we are interested in all data after 2020. We can select these rows in this way:"
      ],
      "id": "81a64885"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select all rows with year > 2020\n",
        "post_2020 = df[df['year']>2020]\n",
        "post_2020"
      ],
      "id": "76c679be",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Let's break down what is happening here. The condition for our rows is `df['year']>2020`, this is a `pandas.Series` with boolean values (`True` or `False`) indicating which rows satisfy the condition year>2020:"
      ],
      "id": "8ffbf83a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Check the type of df['year']>2020\n",
        "print(type(df['year']>2020))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Print the boolean series\n",
        "df['year']>2020"
      ],
      "id": "1d182e2c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        " When we pass such a series of boolean values to the selection brackets `[]` we keep only the rows that correspond to a `True` value. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- TO DO: would be nice to add a diagram about selecting rows -->\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Check-in\n",
        " Get the subset of the data frame on which the San Dieguito Wetland has at least 75 species recorded during spring.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we want to look at data from years 2012 to 2015 (including both years). One way of doing this is to use the `between` method in our condition:"
      ],
      "id": "5759e5da"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "subset = df[df['year'].between(2012, 2015)]\n",
        "subset"
      ],
      "id": "fb02e94c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Let's break down this code: \n",
        "\n",
        "1. `df['year']` is the column with the year values, a `pandas.Series`\n",
        "\n",
        "2. in `df['year'].between()`, we have that [`between` is a method for the `pandas.Series`](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.Series.between.html) and we are calling it using the dot `.`\n",
        "\n",
        "3. `(2012, 2015)` are the parameters for the `between()` method, from the `pandas` documentation we can see this method will subset including both endpoints\n",
        "\n",
        "4. `df['year'].between(2012, 2015)` is then a `pandas.Series` of boolean values indicating which rows have year equal to 2012, 2013, 2014, or 2015. \n",
        "\n",
        "5. when we put `df['year'].between(2012, 2015)` inside the selection brackets `[]` we obtain the rows of the data frame with year equal to 2012, ..., 2015.\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--\n",
        ":::{.callout-warning}\n",
        "## Avoid using `loc` for selecting only rows\n",
        "It is equivalent to write\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "# Select rows with year<2015\n",
        "df[df['year']<2015]\n",
        "```\n",
        "and\n",
        "```python\n",
        "# Select rows with year<2015 using loc\n",
        "df.loc[ df['year']<2015 , :]\n",
        "```\n",
        "In the second one:\n",
        "\n",
        "- we are using the `df.loc[ row-selection , column-selection]` syntax\n",
        "\n",
        "- the `row-selection` parameter is the condition `df['year']<2015`\n",
        "\n",
        "- the `column-selection` parameter is a colon `:`, which indicates we want all columns for the rows we are selecting.\n",
        "\n",
        "We prefer the first syntax when we are selecting rows and not columns since it is simpler.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "### ... using multiple conditions\n",
        "We can combine multiple conditions to select rows by surrounding each one in parentheses `()` and using the or operator `|` and the and operator `&`.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example: or \n",
        "\n",
        "Let's select rows in which the Carpinteria Salt Marsh has more than 50 species registered in winter *or* fall:"
      ],
      "id": "3a2a6fde"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[ (df['CSM_winter']>50) | (df['CSM_fall']>50)]"
      ],
      "id": "5bb2a84d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#### Example: and\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's select rows in which both the Carpinteria Salt Marsh and the San Dieguito Wetland have more than 60 reported bird species during spring:"
      ],
      "id": "6327c0a3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[ (df['CSM_spring']>60) & (df['SDW_spring']>60)]"
      ],
      "id": "3f9ee07c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "An empty data frame! That's ok, it just means there are no rows that satisfy the given condition.\n",
        "\n",
        "### ... by position\n",
        "\n",
        "All the selections we have done so far have been using labels. Sometimes we may want to select certain rows depending on their *actual position* in the data frame. In other words, using **position-based subsetting**.  To do this, we use **`iloc` selection** with the syntax\n",
        "```python\n",
        " df.iloc[row-indices]\n",
        " ```\n",
        " `iloc` stands for integer-location based indexing.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Our `post_2020` subset kept the original row labels from `df`: 11, 12, and 13."
      ],
      "id": "b4a10336"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select the first row by position\n",
        "post_2020.iloc[0]"
      ],
      "id": "6120dc27",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Select the same row by its label\n",
        "post_2020.loc[11]"
      ],
      "id": "87319d3b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Both return the 2021 row. `iloc` counts positions starting from 0, while `loc` uses the labels in the index. That's why `post_2020.loc[0]` raises a `KeyError`: there is no row labeled 0 in `post_2020`."
      ],
      "id": "f6857cda"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
